        "thinking": "Now I can see the full picture. The Express `req.actor` has `type: \"board\" | \"agent\" | \"none\"` (flat union, not discriminated). I need authz.ts to work with both `RequestCtx` (which has `actor: ActorContext | null`) and Express `Request` (which has the flat `actor` object with all optional fields).\n\nThe cleanest structural approach: define a minimal interface in authz.ts that both satisfy:\n\n```ts\ninterface AuthzActor {\n  type: string;\n  source?: string;\n  isInstanceAdmin?: boolean;\n  companyIds?: string[];\n  memberships?: { companyId: string; membershipRole?: string | null; status?: string }[];\n  userId?: string;\n  agentId?: string;\n  companyId?: string;\n  runId?: string;\n}\n\ninterface AuthzReq {\n  actor: AuthzActor | null;\n  method: string;\n}\n```\n\nNow checking compatibility: Express `req.actor` has the right `type` field and `req.method` is a string, so both work structurally. The only difference is that Express never sets `actor` to null—it's always present—but that's fine since the interface allows null and Express satisfies the non-null case.\n\nFor `RequestCtx`, the `actor` field can be `ActorContext | null`, which covers both `BoardActor` and `AgentActor` scenarios, so that works too.\n\nThe tricky part is updating `assertAuthenticated` to handle both request types: Express checks `req.actor.type === \"none\"` while `RequestCtx` needs to check if `actor` is null. I'll need to adjust the logic to account for both patterns.\n\nBy making authz.ts accept the structural interface, I can keep existing Express routes unchanged while allowing new Handler functions to pass `RequestCtx` directly — both satisfy the same `AuthzReq` contract.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
